app('uagm','method','Appendix B.1–B.2, PDF p17, explicitly retains DUET’s 2D branch alongside the added 3D value branch. The 768-dimensional visual and text representations produce candidate distributions; pretraining uses masked-token NLL and expert-action NLL, adding target-object NLL for REVERIE. DAgger supplies shortest-path pseudo-expert labels. Appendix C.4 specifies a per-Gaussian block-diagonal, first-order-gradient outer-product approximation for Fisher information. A finite, well-conditioned log determinant still requires an explicit regularization/rank convention, which the unavailable code prevents checking.')
app('uagm','results','Appendix Table8, PDF p18, reports per-step time DUET1.31s versus full UAGM3.48s and MobileSAM UAGM2.63s. Nearby text says the full method adds1.35s, while the table difference is2.17s; report table values separately. Table11, p19, labels five-run mean±standard deviation, confidence intervals and paired t-test p-values. The very small reported p-values warrant checking the statistical unit and raw paired runs before relying on them; no misconduct inference is made. Appendix E.2’s similar-looking renderings do not mathematically bound the neglected Hessian residual. E.3/Table13 reports small gains when uncertainty maps are supplied to DUET, BEVBert and VER, which supports transferable signals but not calibrated probability estimates. E.4/Figure7 gives concrete premature-stop examples: seeing a sofa before reaching it, and halting outside a room with a hidden target. E.4/Table14 uses20 selected scenes, so its10/10 to2/10 complex-scene result is not a representative benchmark estimate.')
app('uagm','limitations','Version provenance: the local23-page PDF is byte-identical to arXiv2605.26503v1 (26 May2026); SHA256 and the exact downloaded URL are in evidence/01/uagm_version_provenance.json. Appendix F.1 explicitly lists active perception and predictive future views as future work. Noise robustness in Table12 does not establish epistemic/aleatoric identifiability, nor physical navigation safety. The Table11 test-unit question remains unresolved pending independent mathematical review.')
notes['uagm']['bottlenecks'].append(dict(description='Uncertainty is attached to current observations but does not select evidence-gathering actions; visible-yet-not-reached and hidden-target cases cause premature termination.',evidence='AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGMENT: Appendix E.4/Figure7, PDFp21; F.1, p23',kind='methodological',status='Still unresolved in this method; broader literature audit required',followups=['HSGM','Active Visual Information Gathering','world-model methods in assignment02'],confidence='high'))
app('srdf','method','Appendix A.1, PDF p19, clarifies that RxR-English and R4R use recovery to a reference-path viewpoint maximizing nDTW rather than a universal shortest-path expert. R2R and other tasks use shortest-path teacher actions. This distinction is essential when evaluating whether the flywheel preserves route semantics. Generator training updates LoRA layers on Mantis-8B-SigLIP-Llama3; the round3 navigator and generator reuse round2 initialization while earlier rounds restart specified base models.')
app('srdf','results','Appendix Tables14–18, pp23–24, reports R4R nDTW56.5/SDTW44.6 and RxR-English nDTW74.4/SDTW66.3 on validation unseen, improving the cited route-fidelity baselines as well as endpoint success. REVERIE test SR61.4 is accompanied by RGS35.6, below GOAT38.3, while RGSPL27.1 slightly exceeds GOAT26.7. Thus data scaling does not uniformly solve object grounding. Appendix qualitative claims about rare objects, OCR and outdoor scenes are examples, not controlled tests of those abilities.')
app('srdf','implementation','The original SRDF snapshot is README-only. Later HLR/SkillNav specialist implementations reported by assignment03 may support experiments with SRDF checkpoints; their availability must not be conflated with release of the complete original flywheel pipeline.')
app('bevbert','method','Appendix B, pp13–15, samples MLM:HSAP:MSI at5:5:1 for route-following tasks; REVERIE omits MSI and uses MLM:HSAP:MRC:OG at1:1:1:1. Fine-tuning minimizes λL_teacher + L_student, with optional object grounding; λ=.2 for R2R/CE/REVERIE but .8 for RxR, whose non-shortest paths use nDTW-oriented pseudo-expert labels. Transformer text-branch attention is cut during fine-tuning to control expanding rollout memory. The estimated-depth ablation trains RedNet on training houses and retrains BEVBert with those estimates; it is not a zero-cost replacement at inference.')
app('bevbert','results','Appendix E, pp15–17, explicitly records early deviations followed by excessive unsuccessful backtracking, loss of instruction-state tracking, ordinal-room ambiguity, and stopping at a kitchen counter after skipping a required turn. These failures persist despite a metric map and masked semantic imagination. Table7, p8, gives short-term-transformer FLOPs4.5G for11×11 cells,15.2G for21×21, and32.7G for31×31, showing that map refinement incurs a measurable cost.')
app('weakmap','method','Appendix A–B, pp14–15, clarifies that fine-grained, semantic and fused maps actually have different tensor resolutions despite the shared m×m notation in the main paper: fine map64×100×100, semantic map27×48×48, fused map256×24×24. Adam uses2.5e−4; teacher forcing runs30epochs, followed by10 DAgger collections of5000 trajectories and4epochs per collection, with oracle-action probability0.5^n. The released trainer regresses bounded2D waypoints using MSE, not categorical primitive-action CE.')
app('weakmap','results','Appendix C, pp15–16, scores localization by IoU between the top10% mass regions of the predicted and weak target distributions:36.7% full,32.3% without fine-grained map,6.9% without localization loss. These targets remain weak localization labels, not independent precise boxes. Removing hallucination drops SR38.9→37.3 in Table6. Appendix H/p18 reports attributes in51% of instructions and ambiguity in33% of their trajectories, motivating attribute-aware map features.')
app('rxr','results','Table7, PDF p9, trains/evaluates unimodal ablations rather than merely deleting a modality at test time: multimodal unseen SR22.8 averages above the language-only and vision-only models; direction words can still guide actions using graph geometry without visual content. Appendix A/p12 explains247 annotators, separate Guide/Follower workers, pilot feedback and retraining below80% success. The datasheet pp19–21 confirms building-level splits inherited from MP3D/R2R and external simulator dependence. This collection protocol supports quality but does not establish immunity to all annotation or graph biases.')
app('gridmm','results','Section4.5/p9 reports the map footprint growing from roughly10m to20m and maximum per-cell feature counts exceeding600 late in episodes. A fixed cell count therefore reduces physical spatial resolution as explored extent grows, while feature aggregation grows denser. The authors explicitly leave multi-floor handling open. These are more concrete constraints than a generic statement that mapping is expensive.')
app('ivln','results','Table4/p8 separates train/evaluation map regimes and predicted versus ground-truth semantics. For predicted semantics, iterative-map training/evaluation reaches35SR/32SPL/47tour-nDTW, while training/evaluating with a fully known map reaches31/28/44. More map information alone does not guarantee better use of it. The human R2R comparison in the conclusion uses a different benchmark/protocol and should not be interpreted as a controlled IVLN human gap.')
app('topologicalplanning','results','Table3/p8 reports CMTP-BCE with R2R maps at28.9SR/24.1SPL on unseen environments versus26.4/22.7 for CMTP, but the autonomous-map setting has different values. Exploration distance is excluded from SPL; collected exploration is roughly300 times the task navigation distance. Separate binary STOP prediction is therefore prior art, while its total deployment cost and map-availability assumptions differ from online DUET.')

